    <Placemark>
      <name>Seven Lakes State Park</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Seven_Lakes_State_Park.jpg/330px-Seven_Lakes_State_Park.jpg" width="320"/><br/><p>Seven Lakes State Park is a public recreation area covering 1,434 acres (580 ha) one mile northwest of Holly in Holly Township, Oakland County, Michigan. The state park's 230 acres (93 ha) of water are found in six named lakes that include 170-acre Big Seven Lake, Little Seven Lake, and 44-acre Dickinson Lake.</p><p>County: Oakland</p><p><a href="https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/SevenLakes">More information</a></p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#state_park</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>-83.6482326428229,42.81690922980116,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Albert E. Sleeper State Park</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Sleeper_State_Park_%28Aug_2019%29.jpg/330px-Sleeper_State_Park_%28Aug_2019%29.jpg" width="320"/><br/><p>Albert E. Sleeper State Park is a public recreation area on Lake Huron in Lake Township, Huron County, Michigan. The state park encompasses 723 acres (293 ha) four miles northeast of Caseville, close to the tip of The Thumb of Michigan.</p><p>County: Huron</p><p><a href="https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/Sleeper">More information</a></p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#state_park</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>-83.21031362146371,43.98035545212465,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Sanilac Petroglyphs State Park</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Sanilac_Petroglyphs_-_Archer.jpg/330px-Sanilac_Petroglyphs_-_Archer.jpg" width="320"/><br/><p>Sanilac Petroglyphs Historic State Park is a historic preservation area in the U.S. state of Michigan. The state park, also known as ezhibiigadek asin, consists of 240 acres (97 ha) in Greenleaf Township, Sanilac County, in Michigan's Thumb. It contains the largest collection of Native American petroglyphs in Michigan. The carvings were created in the pre-Columbian era and represent aspects of Native American spirituality. An interpretive hiking trail within the park passes along the nearby Cass River.</p><p>County: Sanilac</p><p><a href="https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/Sanilac">More information</a></p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#state_park</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>-83.0177745301325,43.65646240661048,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Lakeport State Park</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Lakeport_State_Park_%28June_2025%29.jpg/330px-Lakeport_State_Park_%28June_2025%29.jpg" width="320"/><br/><p>Lakeport State Park is a public recreation area on the shores of Lake Huron in St. Clair County, Michigan. The state park consists of two units on either side of the community of Lakeport within Burtchville Township. Two campgrounds, beachfront, and camp store are on the north side, and a picnic area and beachfront are on the south side.</p><p>County: St. Clair</p><p><a href="https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/Lakeport">More information</a></p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#state_park</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>-82.49708731595024,43.11933434110893,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Holland State Park</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Holland_State_Park.jpg/330px-Holland_State_Park.jpg" width="320"/><br/><p>Holland State Park is a public recreation area covering 142 acres (57 ha) in Park Township, Ottawa County, four miles (6 km) west of the city of Holland, Michigan. The state park consists of separate Lake Macatawa and Lake Michigan units on the northern side of the channel connecting Lake Macatawa with Lake Michigan. It is often the most visited state park in Michigan, receiving between 1.5 and 2 million visitors annually.</p><p>County: Ottawa</p><p><a href="https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/Holland">More information</a></p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#state_park</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>-86.20022641477924,42.7773220861649,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Sleepy Hollow State Park</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Sleepy_Hollow_State_Park%2C_October-1.jpg/330px-Sleepy_Hollow_State_Park%2C_October-1.jpg" width="320"/><br/><p>Sleepy Hollow State Park is a public recreation area covering 2,678 acres (1,084 ha) in the townships of Ovid and Victor in Clinton County, Michigan. The state park is located off US-127 nine miles southeast of St. Johns and four miles northwest of Laingsburg and centers on man-made, 410-acre (170 ha) Lake Ovid.</p><p>County: Clinton</p><p><a href="https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/SleepyHollow">More information</a></p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#state_park</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>-84.40727540984892,42.938566130062696,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Duck Lake State Park</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Scenic_drive_at_Duck_Lake%2C_Muskegon%2C_Michigan_%2864134%29.jpg/330px-Scenic_drive_at_Duck_Lake%2C_Muskegon%2C_Michigan_%2864134%29.jpg" width="320"/><br/><p>Duck Lake State Park is a public recreation area covering 728 acres (295 ha) on Lake Michigan five miles (8.0 km) southwest of Whitehall in Muskegon County, Michigan. The state park, which runs along the north side of Duck Lake to Lake Michigan, features a large sand dune. The park's Scenic Drive is part of the Shoreline Trail route in Muskegon County.</p><p>County: Muskegon</p><p><a href="https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/DuckLake">More information</a></p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#state_park</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>-86.40747553570637,43.34341516990137,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>P.J. Hoffmaster State Park</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Hoffmaster_parabolic_dune.jpg/330px-Hoffmaster_parabolic_dune.jpg" width="320"/><br/><p>P. J. Hoffmaster State Park is a public recreation area on the shores of Lake Michigan located five miles north of Grand Haven at the southwest corner of Norton Shores, in Muskegon County, and the northwest corner of Spring Lake Township, in Ottawa County, in the U.S. state of Michigan. The state park's 1,200 acres (490 ha) of land include three miles (4.8 km) of sand beach on the lake. It is operated by the Michigan Department of Natural Resources.</p><p>County: Muskegon</p><p><a href="https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/Hoffmaster">More information</a></p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#state_park</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>-86.26609647144842,43.13319814267918,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Bond Falls State Scenic Site</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p>County: Ontonagon</p><p><a href="https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/BondFalls">More information</a></p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#state_park</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>-89.13475673313526,46.40977148360081,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Charles Mears State Park</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Mears_State_Park_beachfront_%28August_2025%29.jpg/330px-Mears_State_Park_beachfront_%28August_2025%29.jpg" width="320"/><br/><p>Charles Mears State Park is a public recreation area in the village of Pentwater, Oceana County, Michigan. The state park encompasses 50 acres (20 ha) on the north side of the channel that connects Lake Michigan and Pentwater Lake.</p><p>County: Oceana</p><p><a href="https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/Mears">More information</a></p>]]></description>
